    "text_full": "Toggle navigation\nSearch\nDONATE\nHome\n \nExplanation\n \nAnswering Myths\n \nVideos\n \nEmail Your Legislator\n \nLetter-to-Editor\n \nVolunteer\n \nMedia\n \nAbout\n \nEvents\n\nState status: AK AL AR AZ CA CO CT DC DE FL GA HI IA ID IL IN KS KY LA MA MD ME MI MN MO MS MT NC ND NE NH NJ NM NV NY OH OK OR PA RI SC SD TN TX UT VA VT WA WI WV WY\n\nThe National Popular Vote Interstate Compact will guarantee the Presidency to the candidate who receives the most popular votes in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. It has been enacted into law by 18 states and DC, which together possess 222 electoral votes. The Compact needs an additional 48 electoral votes (that is, a total of 270) to be activated. \n\nMore Videos\n\nTake Action Now – Tell Your Legislators to Pass National Popular Vote\n\nVirginia becomes the 19th Jurisdiction to Enact National Popular Vote into law\n\n5 of 47 Presidents Came into Office Without Winning the National Popular Vote\n\n63% Support National Popular Vote for President in Pew Poll\n\nCalifornia and New York Can't, and Won't, Dominate a National Popular Vote for President\n\nAlmost All (94%) of 2024 Presidential Campaign Was Concentrated on 7 States\n\nIn 7 Elections, 3 Near-Misses (2004, 2020, 2024) and 2 Second-Place Presidents (2000, 2016)\n\nShortcomings of the Current System of Electing the President\n\n40 States Voted for Same Party in 5 Presidential Elections 2008–2024\n\nThe Daily Show: Is America Ready to Elect the President by National Popular Vote?\n\nNational Popular Vote Has Been Enacted into Law in 19 Jurisdictions with 222 Electoral Votes\n\nRead or Download the book Every Vote Equal for FREE\n\nThe 100 Biggest Cities Have 65 Million People, and Rural America Has 66 Million\n\nSmall States Do Not Benefit from Current System\n\nSmall States Are Evenly Divided in Presidential Elections\n\nAn Average of 3 States Decided Recent Presidential Elections, including 2024\n\nColorado Voters Approve National Popular Vote at Ballot Box\n\nMaine Becomes 18th Jurisdiction to Enact National Popular Vote into Law\n\nMinnesota becomes the 17th jurisdiction to enact  National Popular Vote into Law\n\nBoth Houses of Nevada Legislature Approve National Popular Vote\n\nMichigan House Committee Approves National Popular Vote Bill\n\nVoter Turnout Is Substantially Higher in Battleground States than Spectator States\n\nCongressman Jamie Raskin Talks about National Popular Vote\n\nThe Electoral College Is a National Security Threat\n\nOne Delayed Mail Truck Can Decide the Presidency\n\nSupreme Court Unanimously Rules that States May Require Presidential Electors to be Faithful\n\nHow the Electoral College Works\n\nEqual Citizens Unsuccessfully Asks Supreme Court to Declare Winner-Take-All Unconstitutional\n\nAnalysis of the Congressional-District Method of Awarding Electoral Votes\n\nAnalysis of the Fractional Proportional (Lodge-Gossett) Method of Awarding Electoral Votes\n\nAnalysis of the Whole-Number Proportional Method of Awarding Electoral Votes\n\nHow A Nationwide Campaign for President Would Be Run\n\nAnalysis of Voter Choice Ballot (Unilateral Awarding of Electoral Votes)\n\nRanked Choice Voting (RCV) Is Compatible with National Popular Vote \n\n\"One Person, One Vote? The Untold Story of the Electoral College\" -- a feature film\n\nPresidential Election Results 2000-2024\n\n28 States Received 0 or 1 General-Election Campaign Visits in the Last 5 Presidential Elections\n\nMore\n\nThe national popular vote winner should become President\nOne-person, one-vote\nCandidates should have a reason to campaign in all 50 states"
